<TextPage
  title="Manifesto"
  description="Why FixedR shows you no money: risk is fixed before the trade, results are measured in R, and the number that makes you tilt never appears on screen."
  eyebrow="Manifesto"
  h1="Why FixedR shows you no money"
  lede="Open any trading platform and the largest number on the screen is money. Balance, equity, floating profit and loss — blinking, updating, demanding attention on every tick."
  seite="Manifesto"
  hinweis="Reading time · about 6 minutes"
>
    <p>
      We think that number is the single biggest reason disciplined traders break their own rules.
      So we removed it. Not behind a setting. Not behind a toggle. FixedR simply does not
      display money. Anywhere.
    </p>

    <h2>The number that trades against you</h2>
    <p>
      A floating money number is not information — you already know your risk, because you fixed
      it when you placed the stop. What the money number adds is <em>meaning</em>: this tick is a
      dinner, that drawdown is a rent payment, this month is someone's salary. Meaning creates
      emotion, and emotion is exactly the thing that makes you move a stop, double a position, or
      revenge-trade a loss back.
    </p>
    <p>
      Prop traders know this better than anyone. Evaluations are rarely failed for lack of an
      edge. They are failed in one afternoon of tilt.
    </p>

    <h2>There is no money in the account</h2>
    <p>
      Here is the part most platforms politely ignore: the balance of a challenge or funded
      account is notional. It is not your money. Often it isn't even the firm's capital in any
      literal sense. The only real money in the whole arrangement is the fee you paid and the
      payouts you receive — and neither of those lives inside a trading platform.
    </p>
    <p>
      A dollar display on a notional account suggests a concreteness that simply isn't there.
      What <em>is</em> there, and what actually decides your fate, is your distance to the
      drawdown limit and your distance to the target. FixedR shows you exactly that — in R.
      We don't show you the money because, in this account, there is none.
    </p>

    <h2>Orientation, not just calm</h2>
    <p>
      There's a second reason, and it applies even to the trader who insists emotions aren't
      his problem. An absolute number carries no information about appropriateness. Seeing a
      loss of some four-digit amount tells you nothing by itself — is that your normal risk,
      or twice it? On a new, bigger account you genuinely can't tell anymore; the reference
      scale is gone. Percent and R <em>are</em> the reference scale. The R view isn't just the
      calmer display. It's the more informative one.
    </p>

    <h2>Prop reality is already percentage-based</h2>
    <p>
      Every rule your funding firm holds you to is relative: a drawdown limit as a fraction of
      the account, a profit target as a fraction of the account, consistency rules about the
      share any single day may contribute. None of these care about the absolute money amount —
      and if you trade several accounts of different sizes, the money view of the same trade is
      different on every one of them, while the R view is identical.
    </p>
    <p>
      In R — risk fixed before entry, results as multiples of it — the same discipline reads the
      same everywhere: risk one unit, make two. On the small account and the big one. This month
      and next year.
    </p>

    <h2>"Isn't that just casino chips?"</h2>
    <p>
      The obvious objection: casinos replace money with chips so gamblers feel it less and lose
      more. Isn't hiding money the same trick?
    </p>
    <p>
      It's the opposite, and the difference is where the risk lives. A chip <em>disguises</em>
      risk: no defined downside, no stop, the next bet is always available. R <em>fixes</em>
      risk: before the trade exists, you have declared the exact amount you are willing to lose,
      and a stop-loss enforces it. The casino makes risk invisible so you take more of it.
      FixedR makes risk the <em>only</em> visible thing — the money disappears, the risk does not.
    </p>
    <p>
      A trader who risks one R per trade knows precisely what a losing day costs. A gambler with
      a stack of chips does not. That is the whole difference between a commitment device and a
      distraction device.
    </p>

    <h2>What this buys you</h2>
    <p>
      Calm. Not as a mood, but as an engineered property of your workspace. When the screen shows
      <span class="r-value">+0.8R</span> instead of a money amount, a red day is a data point,
      not a bill. You review a losing week the way a pilot reviews instruments: what did the
      process say, was the process followed.
    </p>
    <p>
      To be precise about what we promise: a screen cannot erase what you know, and we won't
      pretend it can. What it can do is stop feeding the reflex, trade after trade, day after
      day. And it makes scaling a non-event: you build your routine on a 10k account and take
      it unchanged to a 100k — the same R values, the same percentages, not a single pixel
      changes. The habit you trained is the habit you keep.
    </p>
    <p>
      That is what FixedR is for. The features — mandatory stops, position size computed from
      risk, one trade distributed across accounts, the R calendar — are all the same idea applied
      consistently: <strong>decide risk once, before the trade; then let nothing on the screen
      renegotiate it with you.</strong>
    </p>

    <span class="ln" aria-hidden="true"></span>
